# Maps

A map associates keys with values. Each key appears once and has exactly one
value. `Map(keys, values)` builds a map from two lists of the same length, the
key at each position paired with the value at the same position:

```hql
Map(["Dune", "Emma"], [412, 474])
```

```text
Map {"Dune": 412, "Emma": 474}
```

<!-- run: check -->
```hql
Map(["Dune", "Emma"], [412, 474])
```

```text
Map<String, Int>
```

All the keys have one type, `K`, and all the values another, `V`. A map is
not a `Data` record: a record's fields may each hold a different type, and
the program does not know which fields there are.

A repeated key is an error, and so are lists of different lengths. Both are
found when the map is built:

```hql
Map(["Dune", "Dune"], [412, 474])
```

```text
execute failure[invalid-argument]: duplicate map key Dune
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | Map(["Dune", "Dune"], [412, 474])
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

A map is usually built from a list of records, one list for the keys and one
for the values:

```hql
type BookData : DataContract = {
    title: String
    pages: Int
}

raw : List<Data> = [{title: "Dune", pages: 412}, {title: "Emma", pages: 474}]
books = raw | BookData
Map(books | map(b => b.title), books | map(b => b.pages))
```

```text
Map {"Dune": 412, "Emma": 474}
```

Some steps return a map. `count_by` counts the elements with each key:

```hql
["sf", "novel", "sf", "essay", "sf"] | count_by(g => g)
```

```text
Map {"sf": 3, "novel": 1, "essay": 1}
```

## Looking up a key

`get` returns the value for a key. The key may be missing, so `get` returns an
`Option<V>`:

```hql
pages = Map(["Dune", "Emma"], [412, 474])
[pages.get("Dune"), pages.get("Ubik")]
```

```text
[412, none]
```

`unwrap_or` supplies a value for a missing key:

```hql
pages = Map(["Dune", "Emma"], [412, 474])
pages.get("Ubik").unwrap_or(0)
```

```text
0
```

A map is not indexed with `[]`. `xs[i]` reads a position, and a map has keys,
not positions.

## Keys, values and entries

`.keys` is the set of keys. `values` returns the values as a list, and
`entries` returns the key and value of each entry as a `Pair`:

```hql
pages = Map(["Dune", "Emma"], [412, 474])
pages.keys
```

```text
{"Dune", "Emma"}
```

```hql
pages = Map(["Dune", "Emma"], [412, 474])
pages | values | sum
```

```text
886
```

```hql
pages = Map(["Dune", "Emma"], [412, 474])
pages | entries | filter(e => e.second > 420) | map(e => e.first)
```

```text
["Emma"]
```

A map is not a `Collection`, so `size`, `map` and `filter` do not take a map
directly. Apply them to its keys, its values or its entries:

```hql
pages = Map(["Dune", "Emma"], [412, 474])
pages.keys | size
```

```text
2
```

## Merging

`merge` combines two maps. A key in both takes the value from the second:

```hql
ratings = Map(["Dune", "Emma"], [4, 3])
merge(ratings, Map(["Emma", "Ubik"], [5, 4]))
```

```text
Map {"Dune": 4, "Emma": 5, "Ubik": 4}
```

`merge_all` merges a whole collection of maps in order.

## Ordered and sorted maps

A `Map` makes no promise about the order of its keys, so its keys are a
`Set`. Two more map types add an order:

- An `OrderedMap` keeps its keys in the order they were given.
- A `SortedMap` keeps its keys in the order of the key type. Its keys must
  be `Orderable`.

![Map, OrderedMap and SortedMap built from the same keys; the keys of a Map are a Set with no order, the keys of an OrderedMap are a Seq in the order given, and the keys of a SortedMap are a Seq in the order of String](map-kinds.svg)

```hql
OrderedMap(["Emma", "Dune", "Ubik"], [474, 412, 202])
```

```text
OrderedMap {"Emma": 474, "Dune": 412, "Ubik": 202}
```

```hql
SortedMap(["Emma", "Dune", "Ubik"], [474, 412, 202])
```

```text
SortedMap {"Dune": 412, "Emma": 474, "Ubik": 202}
```

The difference is visible in the type of `.keys`. For a `Map` it is a
`Set<K>`; for the other two it is a `Seq<K>`, which has positions:

<!-- run: check -->
```hql
SortedMap(["Emma", "Dune", "Ubik"], [474, 412, 202]).keys
```

```text
Seq<String>
```

Order also changes equality. Two `Map`s with the same entries are equal
whatever order their keys were given in; two `OrderedMap`s are equal only if
their keys are in the same order:

```hql
[
    Map(["b", "a"], [1, 2]) == Map(["a", "b"], [2, 1]),
    OrderedMap(["b", "a"], [1, 2]) == OrderedMap(["a", "b"], [2, 1]),
]
```

```text
[true, false]
```

An `OrderedMap` and a `SortedMap` are both `Map`s, so they can be passed
wherever a `Map` is expected. `histogram` returns a `SortedMap`, so its
buckets come out in order:

```hql
[412, 280, 310, 145, 474] | histogram(100)
```

```text
SortedMap {100: 1, 200: 1, 300: 1, 400: 2}
```

## The empty map

`{:}` with a declared map type is an empty map of that type:

```hql
counts : Map<String, Int> = {:}
counts
```

```text
Map {}
```

Without a declared type, `{:}` is the empty `Data` record.
[[02-data-types/sets-data-and-maps|Sets, Data and maps]] covers the rule.
